PBS Video.js integration

The player uses pinned Video.js 10.2.0 HTML custom elements. The compiled ESM
bundle and third-party notices in vendor/ are embedded by ../embed_assets.py
into both console payloads. Console builds and viewing need no npm or CDN.

After changing player.js, videojs-features.js, recording-preview.js, avc-recording.js, or package versions:
  cd payload/web
  npm ci --ignore-scripts
  npm run build

Commit the source, package lock, vendor/videojs-player.js and license notices
together. Changes to h264.html alone need only the normal payload build.

Audio is a PCM Web Audio channel separate from the native H.264 video element.
The Video.js volume feature controls that channel directly, including on iOS
where native video volume can be read-only. Initial gain and maximum gain are
preserved; the standard 0..100% slider maps to the existing 0..4 PCM gain range.
The separate dock keeps only the audio synchronization delay adjustment.

The playback feature distinguishes user Pause from a decoder stall. The viewer
keeps draining and bounding buffers during a user pause and resumes near live.
The provider must upgrade before the skin imports: otherwise existing skin
consumers can miss the player context during initial custom-element upgrades.
PiP and remote playback remain unavailable because they cannot carry this
separate PCM audio stream. Normal fullscreen uses the player container.

Offline checks from repository root:
  node tests/ps5/test_videojs_audio.js
  node tests/ps5/test_dashboard_recovery.js
  node tests/ps5/test_dashboard_recovery.js --managed
  python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_embed_assets.py

The browser changes do not change capture bitrate, resolution or codec. Console
capture stalls still require investigation independently of player behavior.

Recordings and snapshots (#9, #10)
The gallery opens one preview at a time. H.264 recordings are fetched in 256 KB
HTTP byte ranges and remuxed in the browser with bounded buffers; the console
does not transcode or generate thumbnails. Closing the dialog aborts transfers
and releases media/object URLs. Video.js controls both live and recorded video.
Live playback pauses during a recording preview and resumes near the live edge.
Recordings contain video only, as before. New recordings have a small .h264.json
sidecar with their dimensions, frame rate and frame count. Legacy recordings
use their own SPS dimensions and VUI timing when present, and explicitly disclose
estimated 60 fps timing otherwise.

The destination is a console path under /data or a mounted /mnt/usb0..usb7.
Changing it is blocked while recording and persists in /data/pbs/recording-directory.
Changing destination leaves existing files in their original directory. Deletion
requires a browser confirmation and refuses active recordings. File requests
from the web gallery bind to the directory that was listed, so another browser
changing the destination cannot redirect a pending deletion or preview.

Additional recording parser check (requires ffmpeg/ffprobe on PATH):
  node tests/media/avc_recording.cjs
HTTP management, range, persistence and conflict tests run in tools/test_ps4_host.sh.

Optional real-browser regression
Run the local tests/http/dashboard_fixture.c fixture (the production server
linked to a synthetic Annex B producer) with PBS_RECORD_DIR and PBS_RECORD_CONFIG
pointing inside build/issue-tests/. Generate its input using ffmpeg testsrc2 at
1280x720, 60 fps, libx264 with aud=1, bframes=0 and a 60-frame key interval.
Install Playwright into build/web-player-test (npm install --prefix
build/web-player-test --ignore-scripts playwright@1.56.1), then run:
  node tests/browser/dashboard.cjs
The test uses installed Chrome (PBS_CHROME may override its executable), requires
a loopback fixture URL (PBS_TEST_URL defaults to http://127.0.0.1:18101), and
refuses recording paths outside build/issue-tests/. It exercises production HTTP
recording, destination selection, resolution failure reporting, snapshot and
recording previews, returning to live, deletion and mobile overflow.
